Q. What is AWS RDS? What database engines are supported by RDS?
A:	AWS RDS is a fully managed relational database service that automates time-consuming tasks like provisioning, patching, backup, recovery, monitoring, and scaling. It allows developers to focus on applications instead of database maintenance.
	RDS supports six major database engines:
Amazon Aurora
MySQL
MariaDB
PostgreSQL
Oracle
Microsoft SQL Server
I mostly work with MySQL and PostgreSQL in RDS for web applications, using Multi-AZ for high availability.s

Q. How to connect EC2 to RDS securely?
A:	To connect EC2 to RDS securely:
Place both EC2 and RDS in the same VPC (or peered VPCs).
Ensure RDS is private (no public access).
Configure Security Groups so EC2’s SG is allowed inbound to RDS’s SG on the database port (e.g., 3306 for MySQL).
Use a private endpoint (RDS endpoint in private subnet).
Use SSL/TLS for encryption in transit.
Store DB credentials securely in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store.
Q. What are the benefits of RDS?
A:	Main benefits of AWS RDS:
Fully managed —  maintenance.
High availability — Multi-AZ deployment for failover.z
Scalability — Vertical (instance size) & horizontal (read replicas) scaling.
Automated backups — Point-in-time recovery.
Security — Encryption at rest (KMS) & in transit (SSL/TLS).
Monitoring — CloudWatch, Enhanced Monitoring, Performance Insights.
Cost flexibility — On-demand or reserved pricing.

